-# Female identity assignment policy (0.9.25)
+# Female identity assignment policy (0.9.27)
+
+## Private reviewed identity layer
+
+An optional `identity-local-review.json` contains explicitly reviewed native Tag
+ID/name bindings. The builder embeds it locally; it is git-ignored and must never
+be included in public bundles, diffs or source hashes. Public integration packages
+rebuild with an empty private layer. Keep a protected backup before rebuilding or
+installing. A native ID or exact name mismatch fails closed rather than falling
+back to unrelated canonical aliases.
+
+Identity categories include female fictional characters and verified female adult
+performers, plus explicitly approved fictional mascots, avatars, female forms,
+shared mantles and collectives. A fictionalized mascot is not its brand/company or
+the real person who inspired it. No real performer qualifies without independent
+adult verification. Provenance distinguishes supplied research, user decisions and
+the public registry. Research is not claimed to have been independently rechecked.
+
+`matchNames` is separate from native binding names and presentation aliases.
+Mislabels must not become match terms. Short identifiers require explicit opt-in
+and whole-token boundaries. `associationMode: review-required` always blocks new
+automatic additions even when identity eligibility is positive. It is used for
+mixed tags, uncertain depiction, form restrictions and generic names. No stored
+review marker, Studio, Group, model response or file folder can bypass that hold.
+Existing associations remain untouched. This release has no new mutation endpoint.
+
+Canonical collisions are checked across separate local bindings, not just shared
+record-object identity. Collision read results retain recognized identity evidence;
+ineligible and ignored decisions are not overwritten by duplicate uncertainty.
+Private decisions do not alter the historical Character taxonomy API.
+
+Run the full public fixture suite in a staging copy without the private binding
+file, then run the local-policy fixtures and compare every native Tag against the
+installed read API. Old unit fixture IDs intentionally do not match a real private
+inventory. Never run a live tagging task as a test.
